import{_ as i,o as t,c as s,a2 as r}from"./chunks/framework.DPTp4bo7.js";const f=JSON.parse('{"title":"Reactor Crucible Logistics Anomalies","description":"","frontmatter":{"head":[["link",{"rel":"canonical","href":"https://kokobird28144.github.io/endfield-skydoc/reactor-crucible-logistics-anomalies"}],["link",{"rel":"alternate","hreflang":"en","href":"https://kokobird28144.github.io/endfield-skydoc/reactor-crucible-logistics-anomalies"}],["link",{"rel":"alternate","hreflang":"zh-CN","href":"https://kokobird28144.github.io/endfield-skydoc/zh/reactor-crucible-logistics-anomalies"}],["link",{"rel":"alternate","hreflang":"x-default","href":"https://kokobird28144.github.io/endfield-skydoc/reactor-crucible-logistics-anomalies"}]]},"headers":[],"relativePath":"reactor-crucible-logistics-anomalies.md","filePath":"logistics/src/page-vgX94GkmwkrVct7UWZN3bJ.md"}'),a={name:"reactor-crucible-logistics-anomalies.md"};function c(n,e,o,l,p,u){return t(),s("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[r('<div class="logistics-content"><span class="logistics-source-anchor" id="反应池物流异常" aria-hidden="true"></span><h1 id="reactor-crucible-logistics-anomalies" tabindex="-1">Reactor Crucible Logistics Anomalies <a class="header-anchor" href="#reactor-crucible-logistics-anomalies" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Reactor Crucible Logistics Anomalies&quot;">​</a></h1><blockquote><p>Source page ID: <code>vgX94GkmwkrVct7UWZN3bJ</code> | <a href="https://docs.qq.com/aio/DTmluZ1diWlpQZldw?p=vgX94GkmwkrVct7UWZN3bJ" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Open in Tencent Docs</a></p></blockquote><p><span id="source-block-h3GMS2ZHH1VcDcEQRt1n2o"></span></p><span class="logistics-source-anchor" id="反应池-扩容反应池输出优先级初步" aria-hidden="true"></span><h2 id="preliminary-output-priority-findings-for-reactor-crucibles-expanded-crucibles" tabindex="-1">Preliminary Output-Priority Findings for Reactor Crucibles / Expanded Crucibles <a class="header-anchor" href="#preliminary-output-priority-findings-for-reactor-crucibles-expanded-crucibles" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Preliminary Output-Priority Findings for Reactor Crucibles / Expanded Crucibles&quot;">​</a></h2><p>When a crucible reacts and its output ports are configured, two cases arise: a product outputs first and only overflows into the next reaction; or it enters the next reaction first and only overflows into output.</p><p>Experiment 1:</p><p><img src="/endfield-skydoc/logistics/src/assets/images/img-e6571eee8ddfb270.q90-58fcafc84d8f9bf6.webp" alt="Reactor Crucible Logistics Anomalies"></p><p><img src="/endfield-skydoc/logistics/src/assets/images/img-14fb58952bc1754b.q90-9bc549081f9743cd.webp" alt="Reactor Crucible Logistics Anomalies"></p><p>The Expanded Crucible performs two consecutive reactions:</p><p>Xiranite + Clean Water → Liquid Xiranite</p><p>Liquid Xiranite + Sewage → Xircon Effluent + Inert Xircon Effluent</p><p>Liquid Xiranite is intermediate. Setting its output port produces no fluid output: it goes straight into the second reaction, which operates normally. Its reactant coefficient is 1, so it immediately enters the next reaction when produced.</p><p>Experiment 2:</p><p><img src="/endfield-skydoc/logistics/src/assets/images/img-04165cda70789d04.q90-722da6d6036ee16e.webp" alt="Reactor Crucible Logistics Anomalies"></p><p><img src="/endfield-skydoc/logistics/src/assets/images/img-e1a020b1d73eb88a.q90-5cc63ad8f1964c45.webp" alt="Reactor Crucible Logistics Anomalies"></p><p><img src="/endfield-skydoc/logistics/src/assets/images/img-29ba3042527c4997.q90-64594568f0566457.webp" alt="Reactor Crucible Logistics Anomalies"></p><p>This is a half-rate Xircon line. The upper Expanded Crucible performs:</p><p>2*Xircon Effluent + Ferrium Powder → Xircon + Sewage</p><p>Xircon Effluent is supplied by the lower crucible and has coefficient 2. Setting its output port makes it output directly instead of reacting. Effluent enters the second crucible at one unit every 2 seconds. The first unit cannot react and outputs immediately. When the second arrives, the preceding one is already gone, so it still cannot react, and so on.</p><p>Experiment 3:</p><p><img src="/endfield-skydoc/logistics/src/assets/images/img-883fec225ab608a2.q90-3c9058d991ccb7df.webp" alt="Reactor Crucible Logistics Anomalies"></p><p><img src="/endfield-skydoc/logistics/src/assets/images/img-b2351a76ec9f5067.q90-dc4c56f3e5cc76f2.webp" alt="Reactor Crucible Logistics Anomalies"></p><p><img src="/endfield-skydoc/logistics/src/assets/images/img-3a35c4cc6e5023a7.q90-1285f8cab9a40f8f.webp" alt="Reactor Crucible Logistics Anomalies"></p><p>The upper Expanded Crucible performs multiple reactions:</p><p>Xiranite + Clean Water → Liquid Xiranite</p><p>Liquid Xiranite + Sewage → Xircon Effluent + Inert Xircon Effluent</p><p>Xircon Effluent (internally produced input) + Xircon Effluent (externally supplied input) + Ferrium Powder → Xircon + Sewage</p><p>Effluent for the third reaction comes from two sources: the lower crucible and internal production. Setting an effluent output port outputs it all instead of reacting. External arrival and internal production can almost never be synchronized. Its mean input period is much shorter than the pipe transport period [as stated in the source], so no effluent remains inside the upper crucible to react.</p><p>Experiment 4:</p><p><img src="/endfield-skydoc/logistics/src/assets/images/img-e98761e90cd88b2f.q90-e15ab07dc67c6de8.webp" alt="Reactor Crucible Logistics Anomalies"></p><p>In the controlled experiment shown, store effluent in two Fluid Tanks, configure the crucible outputs in advance, and connect pipes to the tanks and crucible. Select the pipes and move them simultaneously to complete the connections. Xircon production is discontinuous and periodic, at 12/min. <span class="mark-red"><em>Cause unknown.</em></span></p><p>In fact, two control ports limit the input. The limited sequence consists of consecutive 1s followed by consecutive 0s, depending on input: starting with full input always gives the first several fluid units consecutively. For example, 12/min gives [1]*2+[0]*18; 24 gives [1]*4+[0]*16; full 30 gives ([1]*5+[0]*15)*n rather than [10]*n. With simultaneous inputs, the crucible accumulates fluid (output 2, input 4/s), reacting whenever two units accumulate.</p><p>Application:</p><p>Experiment 1&#39;s logic can build the fixed-output “73.75 Xircon” line for 14.75 SC Wuling Battery.</p><p><img src="/endfield-skydoc/logistics/src/assets/images/img-d1ce25a766deca70.q90-8a2135cc6e238298.webp" alt="Reactor Crucible Logistics Anomalies"></p><p>The production schedule is “figure it out yourself”: three full-rate Xiranite inputs enter below. An 18.4/min Liquid Xiranite input at the lower left gives 73.75/min Xircon; 30/min gives 81/min Xircon. The central objective is consuming all three Liquid Xiranite streams.</p><p>The upper and upper-left Expanded Crucibles are visibly below full rate, unable to consume 30/min Liquid Xiranite, and each has a spare output port. Output their Liquid Xiranite and merge it with the lower-left input into the small crucible to make effluent. Since Liquid Xiranite has reactant coefficient 1, it reacts inside the Expanded Crucibles first, with only the excess output.</p><p>This greatly reduces pipe-component count and avoids the heavy logistics-limit usage of traditional exact splitting of Sewage input.</p></div>',1)])])}const m=i(a,[["render",c]]);export{f as __pageData,m as default};
